%erg 0.1
Title: erg-pr-merge regex misses tickets/closed/NNNN paths
Created: 2026-07-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-07-02T07:47Z MinhHaDuong created

2026-07-02T07:48Z migrated from GitHub issue #145
--- body ---
Migrated from GitHub issue MinhHaDuong/git-erg#145 (opened 2026-05-23).

## Problem

The ticket-ID extraction regex in `erg-pr-merge` (line ~73):

```bash
TICKET_ID=$(echo "$BODY" | grep -oiP '^\*{0,2}ticket:?\*{0,2}:?\s*tickets/\K\d+' | head -1 || true)
```

matches `tickets/NNNN` but silently yields empty string for `tickets/closed/NNNN`. When `erg close` archives the ticket before the merge (moving it to `tickets/closed/`), the PR body's `**Ticket:** tickets/closed/NNNN-name.erg` line does not match, so the auto-close step is skipped without error.

## Workaround

Don't pre-close the ticket; let `erg-pr-merge` close it atomically. If ticket is already closed (e.g. closed via another path), skip `erg-pr-merge` and use `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/merge` directly.

## Reproduction

Observed during aedist-technical-report PR #442/#443 (ticket 0237 pre-closed via `erg close`, then moved to `tickets/closed/`; PR body referenced `tickets/closed/0237-...`).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Regex tolerates an optional `closed/` segment, e.g.:
      `grep -oiP '^\*{0,2}ticket:?\*{0,2}:?\s*tickets/(?:closed/)?\K\d+'`
- [ ] PR bodies referencing `tickets/closed/NNNN-...` still resolve the ticket ID

## Imagine

**Scope correction.** The ticket title/body/exit-criteria describe
`erg-pr-merge` (a merge-and-close script) with a `grep -oiP` (GNU-only PCRE)
suggested fix. Neither exists in this repo: there is no `skills/merge/`
directory here (that path is a harness-repo convention, not git-erg's — this
is git-erg itself). The actual defect lives in `tickets/erg-github`
(`cmd_verify`, lines 76-78), a *verification* script that fails a PR whose
referenced ticket is still open. Its header (line 75) explicitly commits to
portability: "grep -iE + BRE sed; never GNU-only grep -oP" — so the ticket's
suggested `grep -oiP` fix is inapplicable here on top of being the wrong file.

**Consequence, corrected.** The ticket's Problem section describes the effect
as "auto-close step is skipped without error" (true of `erg-pr-merge`, a
close-executing script, where a missed match just means nothing gets
auto-closed — inert). `cmd_verify` in `tickets/erg-github` is different: it is
a required CI gate that either PASSES or FAILS a PR. Traced the empty-`$ids`
branch (lines 80-89): when the regex fails to extract an id, `cmd_verify`
does NOT skip silently — it takes the "escape hatch" branch built for
genuinely unlinked PRs, logs `PASS -- no ticket referenced (escape hatch
invoked)`, emits a `::warning::` in CI, and exits 0. So a PR that correctly
references `tickets/closed/0237-...` is misclassified as an *unlinked* PR:
it passes, but via the wrong path and with a spurious "no ticket referenced"
audit warning, rather than via the intended `ticket_is_closed` check (which
already handles the closed/ subdirectory correctly, line 48) reporting "PASS
-- ticket 0237 is closed in this PR." Worse case: if a PR body referenced
`tickets/closed/NNNN` for a ticket that was *not actually* closed (body
written by hand, or copy-paste error), the escape hatch would falsely PASS a
PR that should FAIL — a silent compliance bypass on the required check, not
merely a missing convenience.

**Premise check: LIVE.** Verified empirically (not just by reading):
```
$ printf '**Ticket:** tickets/0237-foo.erg\n' | grep -iE '^[*]{0,2}ticket:?[*]{0,2}:? *tickets/[0-9]{4}' | sed 's/.*tickets\/\([0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]\).*/\1/'
0237
$ printf '**Ticket:** tickets/closed/0237-foo.erg\n' | grep -iE '^[*]{0,2}ticket:?[*]{0,2}:? *tickets/[0-9]{4}' | sed 's/.*tickets\/\([0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]\).*/\1/'
(empty)
```
`git log --oneline -- tickets/erg-github` shows a single commit (`0b9d206`,
ticket 0209) — the regex has never been patched since. Bug is live in the
current tree.

**Simplest fix (against the real file/lines, portable — no `-oP`).** Add an
optional `closed/` segment to both the grep filter and the sed extraction in
`tickets/erg-github` lines 76-78, using a POSIX BRE interval (`\{0,1\}`, not
the GNU-only `\?`) so the file keeps the portability guarantee its own header
comment promises:

```bash
ids=$(printf '%s\n' "$body" |
	grep -iE '^[*]{0,2}ticket:?[*]{0,2}:? *tickets/(closed/)?[0-9]{4}' 2>/dev/null |
	sed 's/.*tickets\/\(closed\/\)\{0,1\}\([0-9][0-9][0-9][0-9]\).*/\2/' || true)
```
(`(closed/)?` is plain ERE, valid in `grep -E`; `\{0,1\}` is the POSIX BRE
equivalent of `\?`, portable to `sed` implementations that reject the GNU
extension.)

**Antipattern scan.** No YAGNI/premature-abstraction hits — this is a
one-line regex broadening on an already-narrow, single-purpose extraction;
no new abstraction, option, or generalization is warranted. The fix should
land as a direct edit to `tickets/erg-github`, not a new helper or config
knob. Recommend the ticket's title and exit criteria be corrected to name
`tickets/erg-github` (not `erg-pr-merge`) and the portable `grep -iE` + `sed`
pair (not `grep -oiP`) before this moves to Plan.
